PAINT(1)                       P32W Unix                        PAINT(1)

NAME
       paint - a drawing made with the mouse: lines, boxes, ovals

SYNOPSIS
       paint [file]

DESCRIPTION
       Under the window manager (wm(1)), paint has a window with
       a sheet to draw on, 480 dots by 300 to begin with, which
       the window's corner makes bigger or smaller. A drawing is
       a list of shapes, not a sheet of dots: it is saved as the
       list and can be opened and gone on with, and the last
       shape drawn can be taken back.

       Under the top line is a strip of boxes. The first six are
       the tools:

       the pen             a line as the mouse goes
       a line              straight, from where the button went
                           down to where it comes up
       a box               its two opposite corners
       a filled box
       an oval             in the box those corners make
       a filled oval

       and the next five the inks: white, black (which takes
       dots away: the sheet is black), and three patterns, of
       one dot in two, one dot in four, and slanting lines. The
       patterns fill the filled shapes; lines and outlines are
       white in them. A press in a box chooses it, and the box
       chosen has a second frame round it.

       On the sheet the button held down draws. The pen draws at
       once. The other tools show the shape's outline, which
       follows the mouse, and the shape is put down when the
       button comes up.

       The last line tells the tool, how many shapes there are,
       and what has just been done or could not be.

THE MENUS
       The words File and Edit on the top line are menus, let
       down while the button is held; the item it is let go on
       is chosen. A file's name is asked for on the last line.

       New                 an empty sheet with no name
       Open                another drawing
       Save          ^O    the drawing, as its list of shapes
       Save as             ... under another name
       Save picture        the sheet as a picture: a PBM file of
                           the packed kind, as big as the sheet
                           is, which view(1) shows and programs
                           on other machines read
       Close         ^X    it asks first if the drawing was
                           changed: y saves it, n does not, ESC
                           goes on

       Undo          u     takes the last shape off the list

KEYS
       p, l, b, B, o, O    choose the pen, a line, a box, a filled
                           box, an oval, a filled oval
       u                   undo
       ^O, ^X              save, close

FORMAT
       A drawing's file is text. Its first line is

              paint 1

       and every line after it a shape: a letter, the ink (0
       white, 1 black, 2 to 4 the patterns) and its two corners
       as x and y, in dots from the sheet's top-left corner, y
       downwards:

              L 0 20 20 200 60        a line
              B 0 40 80 180 180       a box (b: filled)
              O 2 220 60 420 200      an oval (o: filled)

       The pen's is P, the ink and the number of its places, and
       then the places, eight to a line.

EXAMPLES
       win paint plan
       wm -s /usr/examples/paint.hand
              a hand plays it: a line, a box, a filled oval, the
              pen, a shape taken back, the drawing saved and
              then saved as a picture

       It is on the finder's Apps menu.

FILES
       /usr/src/cmd/paint.c       its source

SEE ALSO
       view(1), finder(1), wm(1), win(1), gfx(3)

DIAGNOSTICS
       paint: not in a window of wm, when it is run anywhere
       else. What could not be read or written is said on the
       last line.

BUGS
       There are no colours: the screen is one bit a dot.
       Nothing is filled up to a border: a program in a window
       cannot read its dots back.
       The picture file is drawn again from the list, with lines
       and ovals worked out by paint itself: a dot here and
       there may differ from the screen's.
       A shape cannot be picked up again, moved or changed, only
       the last taken back.
